WEBVTT
Language: en

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:19.700
The Unicode Character Database gives each of these a numeric value. The top row is four ways to write 3; below are superscript two, one half, Roman twelve and the Chinese numeral three. isnumeric() is True for all eight; int() accepts only the top row. This case follows ².

2
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:38.067
A UnicodeData.txt line has 15 fields split by semicolons; the middle line here holds fields 5 to 9. Field 6 is the decimal digit value, 7 the digit value, 8 the numeric value. ² fills only 7 and 8, so its Numeric_Type is Digit.

3
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:52.267
When CPython builds its character tables, makeunicodedata.py sets one flag for each filled field. It calls fields 6, 7 and 8 decomposition_mapping, numeric_type and numeric_value. isdecimal(), isdigit() and isnumeric() each test one flag.

4
00:00:53.967 --> 00:01:12.733
Run over every code point, CPython 3.15.1 with Unicode 17.0.0 finds 770 decimals, 898 digits and 2,023 numeric characters. A decimal always has a digit value too, and a digit a numeric value, so each set holds the one inside it. ² is in the middle set, not the innermost.

5
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:25.867
This code checks the text first and calls int() only if the check passes. The check here is isdigit(). For '²' it returns True, so the next line calls int().

6
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:41.567
Before parsing, int() rewrites each non-ASCII character: a space as ' ', a character with a decimal value as that ASCII digit, anything else as '?', which no number contains. '²' becomes '?', so int('²') raises ValueError.

7
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:55.267
² is inside the isdigit() box but outside the innermost one, which holds the only characters int() accepts. The check passes, and int() raises ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10.

8
00:01:57.133 --> 00:02:14.033
Each row counts the single characters a check accepts and int() rejects; isdecimal() accepts exactly the 770 int() takes. Since Unicode 6.3.0 no new character gets Numeric_Type=Digit. int() also takes signs, spaces and underscores, so to test a whole string, call int() and catch ValueError.
